# Event-Driven Conversion TODO

Ordered task list for converting the Artifacts project from polling loops to
an event-driven architecture. Work through these **one at a time, in order**
— each task builds on the ones before it.

Rules for every task:
- Read `function_map.md` first to confirm you're editing the right place.
- After making the change, **update `function_map.md`** to reflect it
  (new functions/modules, changed behavior, updated "Quick where do I...?"
  rows).
- Don't start the next task until the current one is done and the map is
  updated.

The four polling sites this project is converting away from:
1. `Scheduler.character_loop` — sleeps `poll_interval` while idle, re-polls
2. `TaskEngine._delivery_loop` — scans every order every tick
3. `TaskEngine._auto_convert_loop` — re-scans everything every tick
4. `ConfigWatcher.loop` — re-reads `stock_config.json` off a timer

---

## Tasks

- [x] **1. Build the event bus foundation**
  Create `events.py`: a lightweight async pub/sub `EventBus`
  (subscribe/emit, pure `asyncio` — no new dependency needed) plus
  dataclasses for the domain events: `OrderCreated`, `OrderUpdated`,
  `OrderClaimed`, `OrderReleased`, `OrderCompleted`, `BankSynced`,
  `EquipmentRequested`, `EquipmentDelivered`, `StockBelowMinimum`,
  `ConfigChanged`. Add a new `events.py` section to `function_map.md`.

- [x] **2. Wire the bus into `TaskEngine`**
  Add `self.bus = EventBus()` in `TaskEngine.__init__`. `OrderManager`,
  `Scheduler`, `Executor`, `ConfigWatcher` already hold a reference to
  `engine`, so `engine.bus` is enough — no constructor signature changes
  needed. Update the `task_runner.py` map entry.

- [x] **3. Emit order lifecycle events from `order_manager.py`**
  In `OrderManager.request_item`, `_bump_ingredients`,
  `request_equipment`, `refresh_stock_orders`, and
  `refresh_auto_convert_orders`, emit `OrderCreated`/`OrderUpdated`
  whenever an order is created or its target quantity changes. Update
  the map.

- [x] **4. Emit claim/release/complete events from `scheduler.py`**
  In `Scheduler.claim`, `release`, `complete`, emit
  `OrderClaimed`/`OrderReleased`/`OrderCompleted` on `engine.bus`.
  Update the map.

- [x] **5. Make the per-character loop event-driven instead of poll-driven**
  In `character.py`, add a per-`Character` `asyncio.Event` (e.g.
  `work_available`), alongside the existing `action_lock`/`busy_lock`.
  In `scheduler.py`, subscribe to
  `OrderCreated`/`OrderUpdated`/`OrderCompleted`/`OrderReleased` and
  `.set()` the relevant characters' events (idle characters, or those
  eligible for the changed order). Rewrite `Scheduler.character_loop`'s
  idle branch to `await character.work_available.wait()` (clearing it
  after waking) instead of `asyncio.sleep(engine.poll_interval)`, with a
  generous fallback timeout as a safety net against missed wakeups.
  Update `character.py` and `scheduler.py` map entries.

- [x] **6. Emit `BankSynced` from `account.py`**
  Fire `BankSynced` at the end of `Account.sync_bank()` (and optionally
  `sync_pending_items`). This is the trigger the delivery loop and
  auto-convert loop will react to instead of polling. Update the map.

- [x] **7. Convert `_delivery_loop` from a scan-every-tick loop into a subscriber**
  Replace `TaskEngine._delivery_loop`'s `while running: scan all
  orders; sleep` with a bus subscription: on `EquipmentRequested`
  (emitted by `request_equipment`) and `BankSynced`, call
  `executor._try_deliver_equipment` only for the order(s) implicated by
  that event, not the whole pool. Remove the loop from `run()`'s
  `asyncio.gather` list once it's purely reactive (or keep a
  much-longer-interval safety sweep — note the tradeoff either way).
  Update `task_runner.py` map entry and the "Change bank/pending-item
  sync" quick-index row.

- [x] **8. Convert `_auto_convert_loop` into a subscriber**
  Same pattern: react to `OrderCompleted` (gather orders finishing) and
  `BankSynced` by checking only whether the just-changed material is a
  single-use conversion candidate (`_build_single_use_conversions`
  lookup), rather than re-scanning everything unconditionally every
  tick. Update the map.

- [x] **9. Replace `ConfigWatcher`'s timer poll with a real filesystem watch**
  Swap `ConfigWatcher.loop`'s `asyncio.sleep(interval)` polling for an
  actual file-change notification (`watchdog` if adding a dependency is
  acceptable, or a lightweight `os.stat().st_mtime` diff on a
  much-cheaper idle wait if you want zero new deps) that emits
  `ConfigChanged` when `stock_config.json`'s mtime changes, triggering
  `load_stock_rules_from_file` + `refresh_stock_orders` immediately.
  Update the map and the "Set/tune keep-in-stock minimums" quick-index
  row.

- [x] **10. Emit `StockBelowMinimum` reactively**
  Wherever inventory/bank state changes push a tracked item under its
  `StockRule.minimum` (deposits, gathers, crafts completing), emit
  `StockBelowMinimum` and have `refresh_stock_orders` react to that
  event too, rather than only running at startup/config-reload. Update
  the map.

- [x] **11. Audit remaining polling: `database/base_store.py` TTL cache**
  Decide whether `is_cache_expired`'s time-based check should also
  become event-driven (e.g., invalidate reactively when a relevant sync
  completes) or is fine left as-is (cold-cache path, lower priority).
  Document the decision either way in the map's "Change local DB
  caching/TTL" row.
  **Decision: left as-is.** Guards static game-content catalogs
  (items/monsters/resources/maps), checked once at startup via
  `GameDatabase.sync_all()`, not re-derived every tick like the four
  polling sites this TODO converts. No domain event on `engine.bus`
  means "catalog changed upstream," so there's nothing to subscribe to.
  Documented in `is_cache_expired`'s docstring and the map's "Change
  local DB caching/TTL" row.

- [x] **12. Concurrency/safety pass**
  Review every new `bus.emit`/`subscribe` call for: emitting inside a
  lock you're still holding (risk of reentrant deadlock if a subscriber
  calls back into the engine), exceptions inside subscribers not
  propagating out and killing `asyncio.gather()` in `run()` (mirror the
  existing `try/except` guards already used in
  `_auto_convert_loop`/`_delivery_loop`), and subscriber cleanup on
  `TaskEngine.stop()`.
  **Findings/fixes (see function_map.md for the full writeup):**
  1. `bus.emit(...)` calls made while a lock is held (`Scheduler.claim`/
     `release`/`complete`, the emits inside `OrderManager.request_item`)
     are safe as-is -- `EventBus.emit` is fire-and-forget
     (`asyncio.create_task`, never awaited inline), so no subscriber ever
     runs synchronously inside the emitter's call stack/lock. Documented,
     no code change needed.
  2. Found (not just theorized) a real reentrant self-deadlock: the
     direct, non-bus call to `Executor._try_deliver_equipment` at the end
     of `_run_gather_step`/`_run_craft_step` runs while `character_loop`
     still holds that character's `busy_lock` -- if the same character is
     also one of the order's own `equip_requests` recipients
     (self-equipping), the old code would try to re-acquire
     `requester.busy_lock` for itself (asyncio.Lock isn't reentrant).
     Fixed via a new `already_locked` parameter that skips the redundant
     acquire for that one entry (`executor.py`).
  3. The event-bus conversion also introduced a real double-delivery race
     that didn't exist under the old single-caller poll loop: several
     independent triggers (`EquipmentRequested`/`BankSynced` subscribers,
     the direct call above, the safety-sweep loop) can now call
     `_try_deliver_equipment` for the SAME order concurrently. Fixed with
     a non-blocking re-entrancy guard (`WorkOrder._delivering`, a plain
     bool, not an `asyncio.Lock` -- blocking here would reopen the same
     deadlock class as #2) that makes a losing concurrent call return
     immediately instead of reprocessing the queue.
  4. Exceptions inside bus subscribers were already safe:
     `EventBus._run_handler` wraps every handler call in try/except and
     logs rather than raising, so a bad subscriber can't kill
     `asyncio.gather()` in `run()`. Direct (non-bus) calls into
     `_try_deliver_equipment` from `_run_gather_step`/`_run_craft_step`
     are covered by `character_loop`'s existing per-order try/except.
     Confirmed, no code change needed.
  5. Subscriber cleanup: `TaskEngine.stop()` previously only set
     `self.running = False` and never unsubscribed anything. Added a
     `close()` method to each of `Scheduler`/`Executor`/`OrderManager`/
     `ConfigWatcher` (each now tracks its own subscriptions in
     `self._subscriptions`) and had `TaskEngine.stop()` call all four.

- [x] **13. Smoke test the converted engine**
  There's no existing test suite. Write a minimal manual/async smoke
  test (e.g. `tests/test_events.py`) that spins up a fake/mock engine,
  fires a few events, and asserts: characters wake promptly instead of
  waiting a full `poll_interval`; delivery/auto-convert fire only when
  relevant events occur; config changes propagate without the old
  timer.
  **Done:** `tests/test_events.py` -- a plain asyncio script (no test
  framework in this project), run via `python3 tests/test_events.py`.
  Uses a minimal `FakeEngine` (only the shared state
  Scheduler/Executor/OrderManager/ConfigWatcher actually read/write)
  plus real `character.Character` instances, and constructs the REAL
  `Scheduler`/`Executor`/`OrderManager`/`ConfigWatcher` classes against
  it -- so the reactive wiring under test is the real code, with only
  the heavier bank/API/DB mechanics stubbed out per-test. Covers all
  three asks: `test_scheduler_wakeups` (eligible-only wakeup on
  `OrderCreated` + a timed idle-wait that resolves promptly on
  `OrderReleased`, well under the `IDLE_WAIT_FALLBACK_MULTIPLIER`
  fallback), `test_executor_reactive_delivery` /
  `test_order_manager_reactive` (delivery/auto-convert/keep-in-stock
  fire only for the order/code an event actually implicates, verified
  against unrelated orders/codes staying untouched), and
  `test_config_watcher_reactive` (a real temp `stock_config.json`,
  edited mid-test, propagates via a real `mtime` diff with no timer
  wait and no spurious triggers while unchanged). Verified passing
  (repeatably, no flakiness) in a sandboxed copy of the project before
  being written here.

- [x] **14. Final `function_map.md` and module-docstring pass**
  Update `task_runner.py`'s and `config_watcher.py`'s module docstrings
  (both currently describe the polling design explicitly) to describe
  the new event-driven design. Do a final read-through of
  `function_map.md` for consistency and update any remaining "Quick
  where do I...?" rows affected by the above.
  **Done:** Both module docstrings already described the event-driven
  design in full -- they'd been updated incrementally as each
  conversion task (7 for delivery, 9 for `ConfigWatcher`, 12 for
  cleanup) landed, rather than being left describing the old polling
  design until now. Found and fixed one remaining stale phrase in
  `task_runner.py`'s module-boundary docstring bullet for
  `config_watcher.py` ("pushing changes into shared state on a timer"),
  which undersold the task-9 mtime-diff design as a plain timer; reworded
  to name the reactive mtime-diff explicitly. `config_watcher.py`'s own
  module docstring needed no changes. Read through `function_map.md` in
  full: the "Quick where do I...?" index, `events.py`'s status note, and
  every collaborator's section already reflect the final state accurately
  (they were kept current task-by-task rather than batched) -- updated
  only the events.py status note's closing line (now that task 14 itself
  is done, nothing remains) and the top of this file's own checklist.

